[{:seon.task/name "the-scenarios-measure-real-agents"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the first DeepSeek runs (2026-10-21, `bin/check e2e scenario <k> model`): the scenarios were written against the scripted model, and three things hid behind it.
- A spec with no `:plan` gets `seon.fixtures/decls`' default, which cites `app.m/a`, the task's own name, as if it were a var (`hairy`'s specs a and b). The real agents capped through two refinements (24 calls, 69k tokens) looking for code the plan never named. Correct: the default plan cites the vars the task's proof calls (read from the proof's form with `seon.ref`'s name rule or the reader), so a spec with no plan still cites what to change.
- In model mode, `newns`' verdict asserts the scripted reply's extra content (a dynamic `*depth*`, `ops`, `syms`) that its plan never asks for; the real agent did what the plan asked (proof green, merged) and the scenario still failed. Correct: a model-mode verdict asserts what the plan and proofs ask (the merged program passes the task's proofs and the plan's stated behaviour), and the scripted-only details stay in scripted mode.
- `seon.e2e/numbers`' `:turns-per-merged-task` averages the turns of `:merged` attempts, which are merge tasks with 0 turns, so it always reads 0. Correct: turns per task that reached main, counting the work attempts of each merged task.
Write the three proofs first in `test/seon/e2e_test.clj` and watch them fail; then rerun `scenario hairy model` and `scenario newns model` (DeepSeek, `SEON_PROFILE` not needed: `model` selects the provider; the key is `DEEPSEEK_API_KEY` in `~/src/seon-jolt/.env`) and report calls and tokens against the first run's (hairy 24 calls / 69k tokens, failed; newns 2 calls, verdict failed).

Merged (2026-10-21): `seon.fixtures/decls` cites the vars the proof's form calls (read with `seon.reader/read-text`: the qualified symbols outside `app.proofs`); `seon.e2e/expectation` holds each scenario's check and expected print, with `:model` for the verdict of a real run (`newns`: `triple` answers 9 and its test namespace passes; the scripted model's `*depth*`, `ops`, `syms`, `scratch-x` and reaching-tests stay in scripted mode); `seon.e2e/turns-per-merged-task` averages, per task with a `:merged` attempt, the turns of all attempts of the other tasks on its branch. Reruns (DeepSeek, `bin/check e2e scenario <k> model`): hairy E2E OK, 2 calls, 3,064 tokens (first run 24 calls, 69k, failed); newns E2E OK, 1 call, 1,228 tokens (first run 2 calls, verdict failed). Learned: with the plan citing the right vars the real agents wrote each task in one turn, and hairy then measured 0 conflicts (both tasks wrote the same text), so it no longer exercises the merge ladder with a real model (task `seon.e2e/a-real-model-run-meets-the-conflict-a-scenario-names`)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-default-plan-cites-the-vars-its-proof-calls
                      seon.e2e-test/a-model-run-is-judged-by-what-the-plan-asks
                      seon.e2e-test/turns-count-the-work-attempts}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-real-model-run-meets-the-conflict-a-scenario-names"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by `bin/check e2e scenario hairy model` (2026-10-21, DeepSeek): `seon.fixtures/scenarios` `:hairy`'s tasks a and b carry no `:plan`, so what makes their merge conflict (a writes `(+ x K)`, b writes `(+ K x)`, the scripted replies) lives only in the scripted model's replies; a real model wrote the same text for both, the run measured `:conflicts-measured 0` and `:attached-tasks 0`, and it passed without a hairy merge. Correct: a scenario that expects conflicts states in each task's `:plan` the body text that makes them differ, as `:conflict`'s plans do (`app.m/f` as `(+ x 1)` and `(inc x)`), so a real model run meets the conflicts the scenario is named for. The default plan from `seon.fixtures/decls` stays for scenarios that need none."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-real-model-run-meets-the-conflict-a-scenario-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-attempt-with-no-agent-is-not-reused"
  :seon.task/plan "Found in RESUME's follow-ups (2026-10-22), and picked as step E's first request (design -> implement -> merge, worked by Seon agents on a worktree's own root): `seon.e2e/agents-reused` counts `:seon.attempt/agent` with `frequencies` over every attempt of `seon.task/attempts`. An attempt refused before its spawn has no agent (`seon.task/attempt-start-tx` takes a nil agent), so two refused attempts answer `[nil]`: the scenario reports a reused agent that never existed, and its check reads a refusal as a reuse. Correct: only attempts that had an agent are counted; agents-reused of a db whose attempts are two agentless refusals and two attempts with distinct agents is `[]`, and of one where two attempts share agent `:a1` is `[:a1]`. The proof builds its db as `seon.e2e-test/turns-count-the-work-attempts` does (`seon.test-store/test-db`, `seon.task/create-tx`, `seon.task/attempt-start-tx`). Size: one line in `seon.e2e/agents-reused`, one deftest."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/an-attempt-with-no-agent-is-not-reused}}]
